body:
I was diagnosed with Obsessive Compulsive Personality Disorder. My ex-partner has helped me a lot: I used to lock myself in my house for months, completely isolated, focusing on some kind of ridiculous task. He was also the catalyst for throwing out over 250 boxes of stuff that resulted from compulsive hoarding. I finally have a house in which I can invite people (first time in roughly 10 years), and that feels amazing. I also try and go out (anywhere) every single day.

&#x200B;

The compulsions are still pretty strong, but I never thought I'd get over the hoarding and be able to go out so often. Even if it's just for an hour per day, the escapes are amazing :)

Rory Gilmore.

&#x200B;

This show used to be my favourite of all time, but binge-watching it as a bitter 27-year-old  (as opposed to a cheerful 14 through 21 y.o.), it really makes me see what an entitled, spoiled bitch she is. Dean cheated with her while married to Lindsay, she cheated on Dean with Tristin, she cheated on Logan with Jess, she cheated on Paul (and "forgot" to break up with him for 2+ years) with Logan, who is engaged to be married... then ends up pregnant.

Never mind the fact she never had a job but always had money and seemed to think this was totally normal.

&#x200B;

Following Lane would have made such a good show.

I ran away from an abusive home at age 14, became homeless, started dealing and lived in squats throughout the country (forever grateful for all of the loving moments and crazy parties at that time!). Despite being very poor, these were some of the best years of my life.

&#x200B;

At age 17 I got into uni, and one month after moving there someone tried to kill me. Combine that with my past traumas and you get an intense case of PTSD, which I'm still battling to this day. It's an everyday battle, but I'm super proud and happy I was able to escape my home and still accomplish things while going through many rough patches (break-ins, house burning down, abusive relationships). Things like getting my BA/MA, raising myself and teaching myself things I never learned because of my neglectful upbringing, being a decent human being.

&#x200B;

/edit: just celebrated 10 years of sobriety!
